the first immunizations with the newly  approved astrazeneca vaccine are  underway the shot was developed in  cooperation with oxford university  and british pharmaceutical company  astrazeneca it could prove to have an  edge for some countries struggling to  deploy mass immunizations  the shot is less expensive and it's also  easier to store  many experts believe these qualities  could make the latest vaccine more  accessible for developing countries in  particular  and let's get some perspective on the  story now we have mohammed munir with us  he's a  virologist at lancaster university in  england good to have you back with us  how significant is the rollout of this  vaccine in particular  well uh so if we look really on to the  number of cases those are surging very  uh significantly in the uk  particularly because of the festive  season and also the new variant and we  are heading toward the winter  so all in all uh situation is is is  pretty grim  and i think the only way we can really  get this whole scenario in control is to  have a mass vaccination  and that is not the priority in the uk  to have as many people as possible  to humanize and the government is  planning to have at least 30 million  people above  55 years old including frontline  health workers care homes to be  vaccinated until the easter time  okay so this vaccine is cheaper it is  easier to store but what about its  safety and efficacy i mean was it the  right decision by the government there  in the uk  to rush to start this as the first  country in the world  well that is something is pretty  debatable the reason is that we have the  data that was published in the very  reputable journal the launch  journal but that data isn't really the  one that made the basis for the approval  of this vaccine the data that made the  approval  for this vaccine is only belonging to  the government that is not publicly  available and according to the  government  is like this that if the first dose is  given the pers  there is around 70 efficacy and then if  it is  uh the second dose given then it would  be around 80 percent efficacy with the  interval of uh  three months but but that is something  that we haven't seen the data so  therefore  all is in air and we don't really know  how efficacious would it be particularly  after the first doze  okay that is important to know and  there's another question muhammad  reports now that the british government  is  worried that this will not be as  effective against the south african  variant of the virus tell us about that  yes south african variant emerged before  the  uk uh new variant emerged and both of  these variant emerged independently  there isn't any link except that there  are a few mutations those are common  and we have noticed that south africa  has been the worst hit country  in the whole africa um with the highest  number of deaths and the infection per  capita  so therefore this is certainly worrying  and whenever when we come to  new variant of course we at the moment  we don't have a data that will escape  the immunity established by the vaccine  but we can't simply ignore that all  these variant could  jeopardize not only the vaccine campaign  but also the uh  the restrictions those been put in place  because of their transmissibility so  certainly  these are the things that need to be  looked very carefully in coming days  mohammed monir virologist from lancaster  university in england as always good to  talk to you  germany's build newspaper and the  reuters news agency are reporting that  chancellor merkel's government  and regional leaders have agreed to  extend germany's lockdown until the end  of january  that's ahead of a planned meeting  tomorrow between the chancellor and  16 regional leaders weeks of tougher  restrictions on public life have  so far failed to effectively bring down  infection rates  and the country's vaccination program  has gotten off to a sluggish start  appeals to stay home seem to have little  effect in this western german winter  resort town  a ban on entering the ski runs failed to  deter large numbers of people from  hitting the slopes at the weekend so  many that  police intervened stopping winter  enthusiasts  in their tracks germany's government  together with the heads of its regional  states  must decide on how to proceed with  anti-coronavirus measures  there's consensus that a lockdown will  have to remain in place beyond  january 10th bavaria wants it extended  by at least  three more weeks the numbers are still  too high and we're not yet able to  assess how the situation has developed  over christmas  new year and especially through the  large numbers of travellers returning  home  that's why it would be irresponsible to  relax the lockdown now  it should be extended until the end of  january  on tuesday state heads will discuss how  long the lockdown should be extended for  and whether the current measures should  be adjusted  states with lower infection numbers want  a shorter lockdown  while those harder hit want measures  toughened  we need to act in those areas that are  most affected  in this case that's saxony and thuringia  so we'll be toughening measures for our  schools and child care facilities until  january  31st just as we'll be doing with public  life will be increasing restrictions  even more  a key topic on the agenda is whether  nurseries and primary schools should be  closed  some argue that young children struggle  with online learning  but germany's central government remains  skeptical about whether physical  teaching in schools is a good idea  our political correspondent hans brandt  is tracking the very latest on this  story for us hi hans  we're seeing reports now that uh the  government of chancellor merkel is  indeed planning to  extend the current lockdown measures uh  what more can you tell us  well that's a further confirmation of  rumors and reports  that we've had for several days now  obviously before the meeting between  angela merkel and the 16 regional heads  preparatory discussions are ongoing and  i presume that this will be a leak from  those preparatory discussions  and it is intended to put those regional  states under pressure that might  consider not extending the lockdown for  as long as everybody else  seems to be thinking is correct until  the end of january  in the end the decision will be taken  tomorrow by angela merkel and the 16  regional leaders and those meetings have  in the past  taken hours have been at times  characterized by very acrimonious  debates  so there's no certainty yet that this  decision is going to be taken  even if the reports today uh propose  that  and this all comes amid a lot of  questions being asked about  chancellor america's government's  vaccine rollout and we have a  graphic rather that we can pull up to  show this the country that has  administered  the most doses per 100 people as you can  see here is  israel it's already vaccinated nearly 13  percent of its population  after that comes bahrain the uk then the  u.s and denmark rounding out the top  five but germany has vaccinated less  than  0.3 percent of its population that makes  it  ninth on the list so hans tell us more  about this criticism  of germany and why there's been a  sluggish start to vaccinating  the main criticism is that there is too  little vaccine available to make it  possible to  vaccinate as quickly as everybody would  like  that in fact is a problem at the moment  in the european union only one vaccine  is approved that is the one from  biontech a second vaccine may be  approved this  coming week so the number of doses that  are available to germany  of a vaccine that is contested around  the world  is comparatively small at the same time  there are some hiccups in  the organization of this vaccination  campaign inside germany  it's not as easy to get a vaccination  even if you're entitled to that  to it as it should be but at the same  time i think there is a sense of  uh kind of desperation from some regions  in germany  that the lockdown measures so far have  not worked very well  have not really pushed down the  infection rates and  that the last hope is this vaccination  so those regions that are particularly  badly affected such as bavaria and  southern germany for instance  are the ones that are pushing harder for  the vaccination program to  be pushed ahead to go more quickly to  give it an extra  push dw political correspondent hans  brunt thank you very much  let's take a look now at some other  developments in the pandemic  japan is considering a state of  emergency to slow the spread in greater  tokyo  that move raises more doubts about  whether tokyo will be able to host the  olympics  this summer south africa has announced a  plan to begin immunizations next month  pending a rival of  its first vaccines the country is the  worst hit on the continent  australia's most populous state new  south wales is reporting  zero local coronavirus cases that state  includes sydney where residents can be  fined on the spot if they don't wear  masks  in public

